{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>The voice is the voice of Jacob, and the hands are the hands of Esau.</b> He could not have said 'the voice of Jacob and the hands of Esau,' for then it would have meant that he was judging from the voice that this is certainly Jacob, and from the hands that this is certainly Esau — and this is impossible, for if he is Jacob he is not Esau, and if he is Esau he is not Jacob. But the explanation of the matter is thus: he said the voice resembles the voice of Jacob — and perhaps he is disguising himself in his voice and the truth is that this is Esau, and he makes himself recognizable by speaking in a tone of supplication, to say that he is fit to receive the blessings. Or the hands resemble the hands of Esau and this is Jacob, and he makes himself recognizable by making his hands hairy so that I should think this is Esau. And what is said 'and the hands' (וְהַיָּדַיִם) with a vav — this is like the expression 'and one who strikes his father and his mother' (וּמַכֵּה אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ) (Exodus 21:15), which means 'or his mother'; so too he said 'or the hands resemble the hands of Esau.'" "And if you wish to say that the vav of 'and the hands' (וְהַיָּדַיִם) is a disjunctive vav, we may say that Isaac thought that perhaps some foreign man was disguising himself in both of them — in the voice and in the hands. But in the end Isaac judged with his intellect that the hands carry more weight than the voice — for that Esau would make himself recognizable by speaking in a tone of supplication is more plausible to accept than that Jacob made his hands hairy, or that some foreign man did so. For how could Jacob contrive — and all the more so any other man — to make his hands hairy to such a degree that they would be like the hands of Esau exactly, resembling them from every side? This is what it says: 'and he did not recognize him, for his hands were like the hands of Esau his brother, hairy' (בראשית כז:כג) — resembling them from every side. And who could contrive this without stumbling in some resemblance? Did he have Esau's hands in his hand and take the likeness from them? This is impossible." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted this verse (Genesis Rabbah 65:20) to mean: as long as Jacob chirps with his voice — the hands of Esau do not rule over him. Even though the plain sense suggests that Isaac said this and thought it was Esau, and furthermore, what does this matter have to do with this blessing — nevertheless, this is its meaning: for Isaac's intention was to bless Esau with the blessing of 'be a lord to your brothers' (Genesis 27:29), that is, to Jacob — that the hands of Esau should rule over him. And he set a condition in the matter, to say: only if Jacob does not chirp with his voice in synagogues and study halls, but not at a time when he chirps with his voice — for the voice repels the hands. And he hinted at this by placing the voice before the hands. For one may ask: did Isaac not follow the hands more than he followed the voice? If so, should he not have placed the hands first? For we find everywhere that what comes first repels what comes later, as will be explained with HaShem's help (Exodus 35:2) in the portion of וַיַּקְהֵל, regarding what Rashi explained: 'He placed Shabbat before the work of the Tabernacle, to tell you that the work of the Tabernacle does not override Shabbat' — see there (Exodus 35:2). Rather, it is certain that he placed the voice of Jacob before the hands of Esau for this very reason: to tell you that the voice repels the hands. And which voice is it? You must say: this is the voice of Jacob in the study halls. And with this is resolved what most of the world finds difficult about this Aggadah — for the implication of the verse is that both rule simultaneously, and this is the opposite of the words of the Midrash. And they have offered forced alternatives for this difficulty, but what I have written is correct and precious." "And by way of allusion, he said 'the voice' (הַקּוֹל) with a definite heh — an allusion to their voice in the study halls, where they learn the Torah that was given with five voices (Berakhot 6a). And 'the hands' (הַיָּדַיִם) — the five hands, the hands of Esau, for the word יָד five times yields seventy [by gematria] — an allusion to the hands of all seventy nations that rule over Israel when they do not chirp with their voice. And so they said in the Midrash (see Genesis Rabbah 63:8): Esau — the letters עֵשָׂו are ע' שָׁוְא, 'seventy [nations] for naught': 'For naught I created the seventy nations.' And so explained the Ba'al ha-Turim."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:35.449471946Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}